i remember aged about 13  my dad was at the hospital i was at home  with the other kids and he phoned me to  say  your mum's not gonna make it  and then he said can you tell the others  and that was pretty tough  for a 13 year old to try to contemplate  [Music]  well i grew up in a little place called  oxted  in surrey my dad was a tool maker so he  worked on the factory floor all of his  life  and i remember distinctly that he would  go to work  eight o'clock in the morning come back  at five o'clock  have his tea and then go back to work  six o'clock till ten o'clock in the  evening five days a week so  that was pretty tough on him my mum was  a  nurse before she had children and then  she  um she had a disease that she got when  she was 11 stills disease  which is where your immune system turns  in on itself she was given steroids  which  were new at the time nobody knew much  about steroids  and whereas she'd been told by the time  she was 20 she wouldn't have children  she wouldn't be able to walk the  steroids meant that she had four  children  and she could walk in her 20s but the  steroids and the stills disease  over the years took a terrible toll on  her i remember  aged about 13 my dad was at the hospital  i was at home  with the other kids and he phoned me to  say your mum's not gonna  make it and then he said  can you tell the others and that was  pretty tough  for a 13 year old to try to contemplate  as it happens mum did make it and  she lived with the illness  for a long time but eventually it meant  that  she was unable to walk unable  really to talk she had her leg amputated  and she couldn't do anything unassisted  and that was really tough i think those  two things  the sort of factory floor worker that my  dad was  um and his strong labor views and my  mum's absolute devotion to the nhs  impacted on me and certainly shaped my  politics as a young person and  i joined the labour party just as soon  as i could age 16.  originally i was so into politics that i  thought i wanted to go into politics i  think  my dad and others thought that law was  going to be a much better  profession to go into so i went off to  leeds university  it was an exciting place to be we were  out and about the whole time  i studied law we sort of worked hard and  played hard  and it was whilst i was at leeds that i  started to study and to understand  international  human rights law which in the end guided  me into  my career you became the eventually  became the director of um  public prosecutions um is there any work  from that time that you're particularly  proud of now  the stephen lawrence case stephen as  everybody knows was  murdered in racially charged  circumstances  and for many years doreen lawrence who i  worked with at the time now  a friend and in fact an adviser to me as  leader of labour party  dory never gave up on justice for  stephen and eventually when i was  director of public prosecutions  there was a breakthrough with new  evidence new techniques in dna  new evidence about fibers that clothed  leave  on the scene of the crime and we were  able to prosecute  two of those responsible for stephen's  murder and they're now  in prison and for to see the degree of  justice for  dorian and the family was very very  impactful on me  the other case that um i remember  profoundly was  cases involving violence against women  and girls i was struck by  just how prevalent violence against  women and girls was domestic violence  domestic abuse sexual violence  and also the more time i spent as  the director of public prosecutions the  more i came to think that the criminal  justice system was not set up  to deal with violence against women and  girls at all well and some of the stuff  we looked at  was obvious nine out of ten  women and girls who experience abuse or  violence whether it's at home  or in the street in public  don't come forward to report it to the  police nine out of ten  and the more we chipped away at this the  more obvious it became  that there was some real stumbling  blocks along the way it's pretty unusual  for someone who's been through an  experience like that to want to walk  into a police station and therefore  we worked with others in the field on  sexual assault referral clinics  where women girls could go to a hospital  setting for example to report what had  happened to them  in the five years that i was there we  set about those  changes i became convinced that the  criminal justice system needed  pretty radical change and needed to be  much more focused  on victims you know some of these issues  have been  there for a very very long time and  some of the um provisions i thought  ought to be in the law when i was  director of public prosecution so that's  2008 through to 2013  have still not been done and that's a  huge huge frustration  you actually became a leader in lockdown  and that must have been  an incredibly strange time for you and  personally  it was very odd to become leader of  labour party during lockdown  nearly 12 months into me being later in  the labour party  and i still haven't done a speech to a  live audience  every speech i've done has been down the  barrel of a camera  and that is deeply deeply frustrating i  think it has made the job harder because  without being out and about in you know  town centres in communities in  businesses where you can talk to people  close up  have the sort of conversations that you  would normally have  without the restrictions it is much much  harder i  feel very much like i want to sort of  take the mask off and  and open the throttle and i haven't been  able to for the  12 months or so i've been leader  obviously we've got to live with  restrictions for a bit longer but the  sooner  um i can get out there to live audiences  um to seeing people in their communities  in the ordinary way  the better it will be from my point of  view at times  some people on the left of your party  have accused you of not criticizing the  government enough  during this pandemic do you think that's  fair  i said when i took over as labour leader  that i would offer a constructive  opposition and i did that because  my strong view then and it's my view now  is that in a pandemic when a virus like  this comes along  which has now taken what 126 000 deaths  most people think first and foremost  about their family  and they worry about the health of their  family and their friends  and in those circumstances most people  want the government to get it right i  think it's right to support the  government where they get it  right right to criticize where they get  it  wrong and you know half of the people  have criticized me for supporting the  government at all  half of them half of people have  criticized me for  for criticizing the government so  something tells me that  we probably just got the balance about  right  what do you do for fun but we know boris  likes to build model boxes  uh what do you do no model buses for me  i've got i think two major outlets and  things that happened at home for me  the first is our kids we've got a 10  year old girl and a 12 year old boy they  are  fantastic they're my lifeline the other  thing is  i love football and i play football  at least once a week twice if i can not  during the pandemic of course  i watch football whenever i can and i'm  a season ticket holder at arsenal  and so on match day when i can go meet  our friends  in the pub for a pint and a pizza and go  to the game  and so my children and football are my  fantastic release from my job  what do you what are we feeling about  his team  despite the results because we're  fighting  to get into the top half of the table  just at the moment despite the results  i'm optimistic and i'm particularly  optimistic about the really new  young players that are coming through  you know we got sucker  smith rowe martinelli fantastic  new players breaking through young  players breaking through 